The FFI vocabulary an `eval` seeds from is PROCESS-WIDE, and only the JIT
runs the program in the process that compiled it.

`crates/zeo/src/ffi_vocab.rs` is a `static Mutex<Option<Vocab>>`: a
whole-program compile publishes every `typedef`/`enum`/`callback` and every
struct layout it declared, and a snippet's compile seeds from it. Under
`--backend jit` the compile and the run share one process, so the snippet
finds them. A linked binary is a new process: its embedded compiler starts
with an empty vocabulary, so `eval "class X < FFI::Struct; layout :d,
Direct; end"` refuses with "`Direct` isn't a declared FFI type".

The fix shape, and it is not the obvious one: do NOT serialize `FfiType`
into `.rodata` -- it is a recursive enum with enum member lists, callback
signatures and struct references, so it would need a table format of its
own. The RUNNING program already holds the same vocabulary as real objects
(one `FFI::Type` per name, plus `StructLayout`), so the embedded compiler
should seed from the RUNTIME's FFI registry rather than from the compiler
process's static. That makes the two modes agree by construction instead of
by carrying a copy.

Everything else in the file is backend-independent; the JIT leg is the
default, so this costs the aot leg one golden.
